		<tr>
			<td>
			<p style="line-height: 125%">From:
			<a href="https://www.mark-davies.org/">Mark Davies</a>, Department of Linguistics</p>
			<p style="line-height: 125%">
			To: ------ --------, Brigham Young University<p style="line-height: 125%">
			Re. Scott Miller, College of Humanities<p style="line-height: 125%">
			Date: 18 May 2020<p style="line-height: 125%">
			<font color="#808080">(Note: additions in
			gray, and web links added as well)<br>
&nbsp;</font><hr>
			<p style="line-height: 150%">
			<br>
			Dear ------ --------,<p style="line-height: 150%">
			<p style="line-height: 150%">
			I know that you are very busy, and I appreciate your taking the time
			to read this. I would not have gone “all the way to the top” if I
			could have worked with other administrators like Brad Neiger or
			Shane Reese, but you will see a discussion of this below.<p style="line-height: 150%">
			<p style="line-height: 150%">
			By way of introduction, I believe that you and I talked after the
			meeting where I received the Creative Works Award in 2017
			<font color="#808080">(one award given each year at BYU; recognizing
			resources used throughout the world)</font>, as well
			as after the University Conference where I received the Karl G. Maeser Research and Creative Arts Award in 2015
			<font color="#808080">(1-2 awards given each year, to the most
			productive of the 2,000+ BYU faculty)</font>. You may also know
			me as the creator of the (erstwhile)
			<a href="https://magazine.byu.edu/article/big-lang-at-byu/">BYU Corpora</a>
			<font color="#808080">(now </font>
			<a href="https://www.English-Corpora.org"><font color="#808080">
			English-Corpora.org</font></a><font color="#808080">)</font>, which
			<a href="https://law.byu.edu/faculty/d-gordon-smith/">Gordon
			Smith</a>, <a href="https://law.byu.edu/faculty/thomas-r-lee/">Tom Lee</a>, and others at the Law School
			<a href="https://www.deseret.com/2011/3/14/20179097/u-s-supreme-court-uses-corpus-created-by-byu-professor-mark-davies">have been using</a> for
			several years.<br>
&nbsp;<hr>
			<p style="line-height: 150%">
			<br>
			This summer I am <b>retiring</b> at the relatively <b>young age of 57</b>, because
			of what I believe is a <b>hostile work environment</b> in the College of
			Humanities, specifically actions that <b>
			<a href="https://humanities.byu.edu/person/j-scott-miller/">Scott Miller</a></b> has carried out
			against me since May 2016.<p style="line-height: 150%">
			<p style="line-height: 150%">
			Retiring at the age of 57 means that I will <b>lose out on more than 
			a million dollars</b> of income and pension benefits (which would
			probably constitute significant “damages”, in a legal sense).
			Hopefully this shows how bad things have been during the last few
			years – that I would be willing to give up this amount of money and
			leave my position at BYU, which had always been my “dream job”. I
			would not be doing this if the issues discussed here were “imagined
			wrongs”.<br>
&nbsp;<hr>
			<p style="line-height: 150%"><br>
			Very briefly, since 2016 <b>Scott Miller</b> has done the following:</p>
			<p style="line-height: 150%">
			<p style="line-height: 150%">
			1. From 2016-2017, he carried out a fairly traumatic, (financially)
			<b>high-stakes, 10 month audit</b> of my corpus activities, even after
			my previous chairs and deans told him his concerns were completely
			unfounded. In the end, BYU (via
			<a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brent_W._Webb">Brent Webb</a> <font color="#808080">[VP
			of BYU]</font>) confirmed that his
			concerns were in fact unfounded, but Scott never apologized for
			masterminding this audit.<p style="line-height: 150%">
			2. The audit raised questions in my mind about the <b>long-term
			viability of the corpora</b> at BYU. I emailed Scott, and he confirmed
			that I would have no access to the corpora if I ever left BYU, which
			meant in essence that <b>they would disappear</b> at that point. This in
			spite of the fact that the corpora were the most widely-used
			web-based resource by people outside of BYU (and especially in
			China); see <a href="https://www.english-corpora.org/atbyu.asp">
			https://www.english-corpora.org/atbyu.asp</a>.<p style="line-height: 150%">
			3. In November 2018, I informed Scott that I would be taking the
			corpora off-campus, which was the only way to keep them viable
			long-term. Scott then forced me to sign a <b>Conflict Management Plan</b>,
			in order for me to keep my position at BYU. (<font color="#808080">This
			agreement stated that I would continue to do teaching and research
			even if the corpora were off-campus. This was insulting, because I
			had been in the top 1-2% of scholarly productivity in the college
			during the previous 10-15 years -- even as I worked on the same
			corpora, which were at that time on-campus)</font>. Apparently I am the only
			person in the College of Humanities who has ever had to do this, and
			this in spite of the fact that Scott knows of several others in the
			college who are involved in off-campus activities.<p style="line-height: 150%">
			4. By December 2018 I had negotiated a <b>licensing agreement</b> with BYU
			to take the corpora off-campus. Everyone else had signed the
			agreement, but <b>Scott</b> alone <b>refused to sign</b>. He kept it in his office
			for almost three weeks, and complained to
			<a href="https://innovation.byu.edu/directory/giovanni-tata">Giovanni Tata</a>
			<font color="#808080">(who has always been very helpful to me)
			</font>that it
			“gave away too much to Mark Davies”. This in spite of the fact that
			it was the “standard” BYU licensing agreement; no special
			concessions were made for me.<p style="line-height: 150%">
			5. On Jan 2, 2019 – within 5-10 minutes of “being convinced” to sign
			the license (by Giovanni Tata and others) – Scott emailed
			<a href="https://cwo.byu.edu/">Creative
			Works</a> and told them to <b>&quot;move&quot; more than $10,000 in income</b> from
			<a href="https://www.english-corpora.org/academic_license.asp">academic licenses</a> for the corpora (from universities like Oxford,
			Stanford, and Princeton) to another college account where I would
			have no access to it (and without telling me what he had done).<p style="line-height: 150%">
			6. After I found out in Feb 2019 what had happened, Scott Miller and
			Jared Christensen (College Controller) purposely tried to <b>conceal
			their </b>previous <b>actions</b>. In the end, they claimed that the explicit
			request to Creative Works on Jan 2 had “all been a simple mistake”.<p style="line-height: 150%">
			7. I contacted <b>Brad Neiger</b> in April 2019 to explain what had
			happened. He was only willing to meet with me for about 10 minutes, 
			but he did meet with Scott Miller for more than an hour the 
			following week. In May 2019, <b>Scott Miller</b>
			had a 1.5 hour meeting with the chair and associate chairs in my
			department, in which (among many other actionable comments), he
			mentioned that he was tired of having me at BYU, and that he <b>wanted
			me gone</b> as soon as possible.<p style="line-height: 150%">
			8. At that meeting, he also explained that from that point forward,
			<b>I could no longer receive any support</b> (travel, professional
			development leaves, etc) for any research that might in any way
			relate to the (now) off-campus corpora. Because virtually all of my
			research is related to these corpora, this means that I am now cut
			off from any funding for research. I am almost completely hamstrung
			in my activities, and there is <b>no option other than retirement</b>.<br>
&nbsp;<hr>
			<p style="line-height: 150%"><br>
			Scott will deny all of these points, but he is not telling the
			truth. I have emails and witnesses for every point mentioned above.
			Scott has almost nothing to support his claims (other than his
			“authority” as dean).</p>
			<p style="line-height: 150%">
			<p style="line-height: 150%">
			Scott will say that these are just tired, old accusations from a
			paranoid, disgruntled, prima donna. However, it is strange that no
			other problems like these ever happened with previous deans. (You
			can ask <a href="https://avp.byu.edu/jrosenberg">John Rosenberg</a>, who was
			dean of the College of Humanities from 2005-2015, if he ever had any
			issues with me; we got along extremely well). The problems only started when Scott Miller became dean in
			2015.<p style="line-height: 150%">
			<p style="line-height: 150%">
			What has made this even more difficult is that almost <b>no one</b> from
			the ASB <font color="#808080">(BYU Administrative Building)</font>
			<b>has been willing to help</b>. (I should say that
			<a href="https://avp.byu.edu/lhowell">Larry Howell</a> is
			the one exception; he has been great.) <b>
			<a href="https://avp.byu.edu/bneiger">Brad Neiger</a></b> showed no
			interest in seeing any evidence to support my claims. He did meet
			with Scott in April 2019; Scott convinced him that I was a “troublemaker”, and
			that was that. Brad Neiger also formally declined to purse any
			action after April 2019, even when #7-8 above occurred. When I mentioned the
			“misplaced” $10,000 to <b>
			<a href="https://finserve.byu.edu/treasury">David Paul</a></b> (University Treasurer) in a
			meeting on 28 March 2019, he told me bluntly to not “air my dirty
			laundry” to him, and he refused to get involved. And <b>
			<a href="https://avp.byu.edu/cshanereese">Shane Reese</a></b>
			never even acknowledged the email that I sent to him in January
			2020, in which I explained #1-8 above. Although I don’t know whether
			this combined lack of inaction would constitute “malfeasance” in a
			strictly legal sense, it seems like that is an apt description in
			practical terms for what has happened.<p style="line-height: 150%">
			<p style="line-height: 150%">
			Apparently <b>Scott Miller</b> will be
			<a href="https://universe.byu.edu/2020/05/19/byu-announces-dean-appointments/">reappointed</a> as dean for another five
			years (2020-2025). But the manner in which BYU did (or rather did
			not) ask for meaningful feedback from faculty in the college has
			been a <b>travesty</b>. When Shane Reese contacted faculty in the college
			to ask for feedback in Jan 2020, he also mentioned at that time that
			they had already decided to reappoint Scott Miller. Shane Reese
			never acknowledged comments from people like me, and he cancelled
			appointments with people like --- ----- and --- ------- (associate
			chairs in my department), who wanted to talk to him in February 2019 about other
			“issues” with Scott Miller. <p style="line-height: 150%">
			<p style="line-height: 150%">
			In other colleges (such as the College of Education), there are
			annual reviews of the deans and associate deans. In the College of
			Humanities, there is no meaningful review – ever. Scott
			Miller knows this, and he knows that this means that he can create
			an extremely abusive work environment – with no repercussions
			whatsoever.<br>
&nbsp;<hr>
			<p style="line-height: 150%">
			<b><br>
			When I came to BYU</b> in 2003 (after twelve years at a university in
			Illinois), I was <b>so excited</b> to contribute to the wonderful
			environment here at BYU. And for the next twelve years (2003-2015),
			it was everything I had hoped for. But the last five years (during
			the time that Scott Miller has been chair) have been a nightmare.
			Not the least because of the almost complete lack of help or support
			from anyone at the ASB.<p style="line-height: 150%">
			<p style="line-height: 150%">
			<b>I leave BYU</b> a completely <b>demoralized and disillusioned</b> faculty member.
			(So maybe Scott Miller is right about the “disgruntled” part after
			all.) I never thought that my career would end this way. But at
			least I am fulfilling Scott’s hope – mentioned to the chair and
			associate chairs from my department in May 2019 – that he could get
			me to leave BYU as soon as possible. (BTW, a lawyer friend of mine
			said that such sentiments – shared with others in an official
			administrative capacity – might constitute grounds for legal action.
			Although I’m not inclined to do that, these actions do seem very
			questionable.)<p style="line-height: 150%">
			<p style="line-height: 150%">
			Although I will be gone from BYU after July 2020, as I leave I would
			like to suggest the following:<p style="line-height: 150%">
			<p style="line-height: 150%">
			1) There be some meaningful annual review of the dean of the College
			of Humanities, and<p style="line-height: 150%">
			2) There be some review of how the ASB (especially Faculty
			Relations) fails to address concerns from faculty (and just “closes
			ranks” to protect administrators such as college deans). 
			<p style="line-height: 150%">
			<p style="line-height: 150%">
			Hopefully these minimal efforts can help to ensure that what has
			happened to me won’t ever happen to anyone else.<br>
&nbsp;<p style="line-height: 150%">
			<p style="line-height: 150%">
			Sincerely,<p style="line-height: 150%">
			<p style="line-height: 150%">
			Mark Davies<p style="line-height: 150%">
			Department of Linguistics<p>
&nbsp;</td>
		</tr>
